a256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{{x}^{3}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x-3){x}^{3}-3({x}^{2}-3x+3){x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-3{x}^{2}+9x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $x=3$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{(1-3)^{2}}{{1}^{4}}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{(4-3)^{2}}{{4}^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{256}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=3.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=3$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $(0,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75475ea4b7463cabcd8545f73218469ca3eaf032b72cf1e124f044def2dbf532" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local maximum at $x=-3$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local maximum at $x=3$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local minimum at $x=-3$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local minimum at $x=3$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/example-3727.json" "file_sha256" "b14506a4c338bfe3e67ec003fceef852f02354db8bb254ced8de9d74f3c96c24" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/example-3727.json" "file_sha256" "b14506a4c338bfe3e67ec003fceef852f02354db8bb254ced8de9d74f3c96c24" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/example-3730.json" "file_sha256" "6dac08692757d9d4c92988a585c59f282242ded62cc37de5cae74a1f27af0c36" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/example-3730.json" "file_sha256" "6dac08692757d9d4c92988a585c59f282242ded62cc37de5cae74a1f27af0c36" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the stationary points of the function $f(x) =x^3$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/example-3729.json" "file_sha256" "9b4f7d8cb9c570e71ecedda4ba4aed81425ebc4f1cc8825d9754ab191f5aa95c" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/example-3729.json" "file_sha256" "9b4f7d8cb9c570e71ecedda4ba4aed81425ebc4f1cc8825d9754ab191f5aa95c" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-78978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-12x+4$ have a local minimum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-4)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =3((-3)^{2}-4)=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3((0)^{2}-4)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3((3)^{2}-4)=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-78978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local minima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+1$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-30x+24.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-30x+24=0\\,⟹\\,x=1,\\,4.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2,5$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =6(0)^{2}-30(0)+24=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2)^{2}-30(2)+24=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =6(5)^{2}-30(5)+24=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |  | $5$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=4$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-344287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=18x-2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18-6{x}^{2}-12x \\\\  & =-6({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =-6(x-1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =-6(-4-1)(-4+3)=-30<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-1)(0+3)=18>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-6(2-1)(2+3)=-30<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-78981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-78981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-2x \\\\  & =2x(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x-1)\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,\\frac{1}{4},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)-1)=8>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =2(\\frac{1}{4})[3(\\frac{1}{4})-1]=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)-1)=4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-113756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+27x+4$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+27=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =-3(-4)^{2}+27=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0)^{2}+27=27>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-3(4)^{2}+27=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-344262" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344262" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{x}$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x)\\cdot {e}^{x}-{e}^{x}=-x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{1}=-e<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}=-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-(-1)^{2}/2}={e}^{-1/2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{-(1)^{2}/2}=-{e}^{-1/2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-e"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "e"] ["math" "{e}^{-1}"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1+x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\cdot (1+x)-\\sqrt{x}}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)-2x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}{(1+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{2}}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1-2}{2\\sqrt{2}(1+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{18\\sqrt{2}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-49027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-2x\\,=\\,2x(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}-1)\\,=\\,-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{2}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{(1)^{2}+1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=3$ is a local minimum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x=0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{3}}{{e}^{-1}}=4e>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{3(4)^{2}-(4)^{3}}{{e}^{4}}=-\\frac{16}{{e}^{4}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-49026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-49026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{{x}^{3}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x-3){x}^{3}-3({x}^{2}-3x+3){x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-3{x}^{2}+9x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $x=3$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{(1-3)^{2}}{{1}^{4}}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{(4-3)^{2}}{{4}^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{256}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=3.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=3$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $(0,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=-3$"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=3$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=-3$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=3$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x{e}^{x}-({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{{e}^{0}}=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{(2-1)^{2}}{{e}^{2}}=-{e}^{-2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-113767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=1$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{9}-9,$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=9{x}^{8}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9{x}^{8}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =9(-1)^{8}=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =9(1)^{8}=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=1$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "b14492e4acb85ad5cf2e01b69763d99de72ad9decc25e0d5212d6a155bf09f17"} {"question" "e-3727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/example-3727.json" "file_sha256" "b14506a4c338bfe3e67ec003fceef852f02354db8bb254ced8de9d74f3c96c24"} {"question" "e-3727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/example-3727.json" "file_sha256" "b14506a4c338bfe3e67ec003fceef852f02354db8bb254ced8de9d74f3c96c24"} {"question" "e-3730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/example-3730.json" "file_sha256" "6dac08692757d9d4c92988a585c59f282242ded62cc37de5cae74a1f27af0c36"} {"question" "e-3730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/example-3730.json" "file_sha256" "6dac08692757d9d4c92988a585c59f282242ded62cc37de5cae74a1f27af0c36"} {"question" "e-3729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the stationary points of the function $f(x) =x^3$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/example-3729.json" "file_sha256" "9b4f7d8cb9c570e71ecedda4ba4aed81425ebc4f1cc8825d9754ab191f5aa95c"} {"question" "e-3729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/example-3729.json" "file_sha256" "9b4f7d8cb9c570e71ecedda4ba4aed81425ebc4f1cc8825d9754ab191f5aa95c"}] "usage" {"q-113768" [] "e-3730" [] "e-3729" [] "q-49033" [] "q-344287" [